---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="05-lists"
  lessonSlug="00-example-list-basics"
  hook="Five tiny operations every later lesson reuses. Index, slice, mutate, copy, len."
>
  <h2>The five functions</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def first_element(t):     return t[0]
def last_element(t):      return t[-1]
def first_three(t):       return t[0:3]
def length(t):            return len(t)
def with_first_replaced(t, new_value):
    copy = t[:]
    copy[0] = new_value
    return copy

numbers = [42, 13, 7, 99, 100]
print("First :", first_element(numbers))
print("Last  :", last_element(numbers))
print("First3:", first_three(numbers))
print("Length:", length(numbers))
print("Replaced:", with_first_replaced(numbers, 1000))
print("Original:", numbers, "(unchanged)")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="The t[:] copy idiom (TP §10.11)">
    <p>
      <code>copy = t[:]</code> is a full slice — it creates a new list with
      the same elements. Modifying <code>copy</code> doesn't touch
      <code>t</code>. Without this line, <code>with_first_replaced</code>
      would betray its caller by mutating in place.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Negative indexing</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`t = [42, 13, 7, 99, 100]
#  idx:  0   1   2   3    4
#  neg: -5  -4  -3  -2   -1
print(t[0], t[-1])           # 42  100
print(t[-2], t[-3])          # 99  7

# Out of range raises IndexError:
try:
    t[5]
except IndexError as e:
    print("error:", e)`}
  />
</Lecture>
